WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:02.479
INTRO:
Since the pandemic started, the money supply

00:00:02.479 --> 00:00:07.060
within the US has risen by $6.3 trillion.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:14.639
This is equivalent to a monthly increase of
$233 billion, a daily increase of $7.7 billion,

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:17.610
and an hourly increase of $324 million.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:22.660
Clearly, the money printing and quantitative
easing has gotten way out of hand over the

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:27.360
past two years, and now, we’re facing the
disastrous consequences of these economic

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.540
policies.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:35.040
Last month, we say an inflation reading of
8.6% which is the highest reading in 40 years.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:40.790
Meanwhile, average gas prices across the US
have rocketed to $4.50, and I don’t think

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:42.530
we even need to discuss housing.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:46.489
To make things worse, it seems like its going
to take several months if not a few years

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:48.239
for inflation to cool down.

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:52.390
But, even if inflation where to go to zero
tomorrow, all of the price hikes that we’ve

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.530
already seen are here the stay.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:59.120
For prices to actually go down, we would need
deflation or some radical change within supply

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.230
and demand.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:03.850
So, chances are, prices will likely just continue
to go up.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:07.170
But, why did the fed even allow for this?

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:11.210
Many just assume that the fed is filled with
a bunch of idiots who don’t what they’re

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:14.200
doing, but I don’t think this is completely
true.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.310
The fed has the best economists in the world
on their side, so it’s very likely that

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.750
the fed did consider this outcome.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.909
They simply decided that the potential consequences
were worth the risk.

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:29.860
But, why do we even have the fed in the first
place, and why do they print money.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.440
It seems like things were way better before
the fed was even a thing.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:39.159
In fact, between 1776 and 1902, there was
literally no inflation.

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:44.980
But, eversince the fed was created in 1913,
the US dollar has only gone down in value.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:49.970
In fact, the US dollar has lost 98% of its
purchasing power since the fed was created,

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:50.970
and that was as of 2010.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:54.900
The number is probably over 99% today.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:01.040
Clearly, this is no coincidence, so why don’t
we just close down the fed and stop printing

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.620
money?

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:08.780
NATIONAL BANKS:
Well, to really understand why the fed is

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:12.550
even a thing, we have to take a look back
at history and see how things were before

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:13.550
the fed.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:17.540
The fed is actually not the first time that
the US has experimented with the idea of a

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:18.540
national bank.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.650
In fact, the US has played with national banking
since the late 1700s.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:28.620
At the time, the national government was extremely
weak politically and economically.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:32.260
The vast majority of power was given to state
governments so that they could better tend

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:33.420
to local needs.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.580
While this might sound great at first glance,
this made the national government useless.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:42.150
They weren’t able to achieve national goals
such as paying off war debt, stabilizing the

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:44.360
currency, and rebooting the economy.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.290
So, the founding fathers eventually decided
to replace the Articles of Confederation,

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:52.910
which was the original document that governed
the US, with the new and improved US constitution.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:57.520
This gave the national government substantially
more power, and treasury secretary Alexander

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:01.280
Hamilton wanted to leverage this power to
establish a national bank.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.690
He felt that a national bank would help establish
economic stability within the US, and while

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:10.300
he did face some opposition, he would eventually
get his wish in February of 1791 when President

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.640
Washington signed the bill into law.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.170
Several months later in December of 1791,
the first Bank of the US would open for business.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.750
At first, the bank primarily dealt with national
matters.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:24.940
For example, they collected tax revenue, distributed
funds across the US, and paid for various

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:26.730
programs and loans.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:30.780
Something that was off limits, however, was
buying US government bonds.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:35.110
The founding fathers feared that this was
not only an inflation risk but that this would

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:37.860
cause the public to lose trust in the national
bank.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:42.400
This also meant that the national bank did
not set monetary policy and they did not act

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.680
as a bail out service for dying institutions.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:48.220
They were just a regular old bank that happened
to be used by the national government as well

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:49.760
as the public.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.409
Despite this, the existence of a national
bank was still a hot matter for the two decades.

00:03:54.409 --> 00:03:58.269
Most of the people who initially opposed the
bank still opposed it in 1811 when the bank

00:03:58.269 --> 00:03:59.720
was up for recharter.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:03.040
And given that the federalist party had lost
the white house, the bank would eventually

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.280
be shut down in 1811.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:10.000
Just one year after this, however, the war
of 1812 would begin which once again introduced

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.580
economic instability.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:15.230
This gave supporters of the bank the perfect
opportunity to win over public support and

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:19.349
establish yet another national bank called
the second national bank of the US.

00:04:19.349 --> 00:04:22.043
Over the next decade, this bank would play
a monumental role in the United States’

00:04:22.043 --> 00:04:25.930
westward expansion as they issued loans to
farmers and businesses that were looking to

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:27.499
settle the west.

00:04:27.499 --> 00:04:30.949
Despite this major role in westward expansion,
they were still not allowed to set monetary

00:04:30.949 --> 00:04:33.589
policy or bail out institutions directly.

00:04:33.589 --> 00:04:37.270
However, it didn’t take long for the bank
to start playing with lending policies to

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:40.029
influence national interest rates and the
money supply.

00:04:40.029 --> 00:04:44.340
This freedom didn’t last long though as
Andrew Jackson would soon come to power.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:50.650
THE ERA WITHOUT A FED:
Andrew Jackson was a major opponent to banks

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:53.830
in general because he simply didn’t trust
them.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:57.650
About 20 years before becoming president,
he sold some land and the buyers would pay

00:04:57.650 --> 00:04:59.970
him using paper notes from a bank.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:04.119
But, before he could trade in the paper notes,
the issuers would go bankrupt which meant

00:05:04.119 --> 00:05:06.319
that his paper notes were worthless.

00:05:06.319 --> 00:05:09.979
In other words, he had essentially given away
the land for free.

00:05:09.979 --> 00:05:14.789
For obvious reasons, Jackson was not a big
fan of banks following this experience, and

00:05:14.789 --> 00:05:17.729
he believed that only silver and gold should
be used for transactions.

00:05:17.729 --> 00:05:21.939
But, this wasn’t the only reason that Jackson
was opposed to a national bank.

00:05:21.939 --> 00:05:25.439
He felt that a national bank gave too much
power to too few people.

00:05:25.439 --> 00:05:29.599
He also felt that a national bank gave the
federal government too much power over state

00:05:29.599 --> 00:05:30.749
governments.

00:05:30.749 --> 00:05:35.009
And on top of this, he felt that the national
bank didn’t have enough regulatory oversight.

00:05:35.009 --> 00:05:40.000
So, from his perspective, the solution was
clear, the national bank had to go.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.550
And when he won the 1832 election, he felt
that he had affirmation from the public that

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.629
that’s what they wanted as well.

00:05:46.629 --> 00:05:50.999
Jackson would veto the recharting of the national
bank, and though congress tried to override

00:05:50.999 --> 00:05:53.599
his veto, they would not succeed.

00:05:53.599 --> 00:05:57.240
Jackson would pay off the entire national
debt by selling federally owned land and cutting

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:58.849
spending substantially.

00:05:58.849 --> 00:06:02.749
He would divide the remaining reserves amongst
the state banks, and with that, the US would

00:06:02.749 --> 00:06:05.189
enter a period with no national bank.

00:06:05.189 --> 00:06:09.910
This actually worked out great for quite some
time, and it seemed that there no need for

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:10.910
a national bank.

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:15.469
But, eventually, the civil war would role
around which not only devastated the economy

00:06:15.469 --> 00:06:17.970
but resulted in loads of debt.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:21.719
And this time, there was no national bank
to maintain stability and keep debt under

00:06:21.719 --> 00:06:24.569
control which led to banking panics becoming
rampant.

00:06:24.569 --> 00:06:28.089
If you’re not familiar with what a banking
panic is, it’s basically when a bunch of

00:06:28.089 --> 00:06:30.889
people become fearful that their bank is not
liquid enough.

00:06:30.889 --> 00:06:34.279
So, they go to withdraw all of their money
while the still can.

00:06:34.279 --> 00:06:39.069
And when a bunch of people do this, it results
in the bank actually becoming illiquid.

00:06:39.069 --> 00:06:41.749
The US saw such panics almost every decade.

00:06:41.749 --> 00:06:45.819
There was one in 1873, one in 1893, and one
in 1907.

00:06:45.819 --> 00:06:49.939
There was also two close calls in 1884 and
1890.

00:06:49.939 --> 00:06:53.839
During this time period, people lived in constant
fear that their bank could collapse at any

00:06:53.839 --> 00:06:54.839
time.

00:06:54.839 --> 00:06:58.319
And it was more popular than ever to just
store gold under your mattress.

00:06:58.319 --> 00:07:02.770
The US put up with this uncertainty for several
decades, but the 1907 banking panic would

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:04.620
end up being the last straw.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:08.349
The public was sick of living in constant
fear of a financial collapse, and the government

00:07:08.349 --> 00:07:10.900
was sick of constantly dealing with econoimc
instability.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:15.509
So, the government got together and hashed
out the details of a new national bank.

00:07:15.509 --> 00:07:19.659
And eventually, in 1913, they would pass the
Federal Reserve Act which established the

00:07:19.659 --> 00:07:22.500
fed that we all love and hate today.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:28.059
WHY PRINTING MONEY?:
As we can see from history, there needs to

00:07:28.059 --> 00:07:32.379
be some sort of central financial body to
ensure national financial stability.

00:07:32.379 --> 00:07:37.219
This isn’t to say that a decentralized system
is impossible, but thus far, a centralized

00:07:37.219 --> 00:07:40.180
stystem has simply been far easier to establish
and maintain.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:46.139
So alright, the existence of the fed makes
sense, but why are they so reckless with inflation?

00:07:46.139 --> 00:07:50.149
I mean, this is literally the third time that
we’re seeing high inflation within the past

00:07:50.149 --> 00:07:51.149
100 years.

00:07:51.149 --> 00:07:55.659
Well, the thing to keep in mind is that the
fed is not trying to eliminate inflation by

00:07:55.659 --> 00:07:56.659
any means.

00:07:56.659 --> 00:07:59.050
In fact, they want inflation.

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:02.830
Not 8 or 10% inflation, but 2 or 3% inflation.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:06.809
So whenever we’re under that target rate,
like we were during the covid recession, the

00:08:06.809 --> 00:08:09.029
fed will lower rates and print money.

00:08:09.029 --> 00:08:12.580
And whenever we’re above that target rate
like we are right now, the fed will increase

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:14.620
rates and engage in monetary tightening.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:18.840
But, why even play this difficult balancing
act and artificially create inflation.

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:23.749
Well, there’s a few reasons for this starting
with encouraging spending and investing.

00:08:23.749 --> 00:08:26.999
Every year that you simply hold cash in a
savings account, you’re basically penalized

00:08:26.999 --> 00:08:28.479
2 to 3%.

00:08:28.479 --> 00:08:30.949
This discourages people from hoarding cash
in a bank account.

00:08:30.949 --> 00:08:34.919
Instead, they end up either spending the money
or investing the money.

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:38.649
The government doesn't really care which one
you do because both of them help the economy

00:08:38.649 --> 00:08:39.820
and gdp.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:43.949
There’s actually a full on economic theory
surrounding this phenomenon called the paradox

00:08:43.949 --> 00:08:44.959
of thrift.

00:08:44.959 --> 00:08:49.300
The theory states that rising personal savings
is detrimental to economic growth.

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:53.100
Here’s the thing, companies and industries
become more efficient and productive with

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.100
time.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:57.589
This means that the cost of goods are naturally
deflationary over the long term.

00:08:57.589 --> 00:09:02.839
I mean, just think about how much TV and computer
prices have come down over the past 30 years.

00:09:02.839 --> 00:09:06.770
I bet most of us wouldn’t buy an 8k TV right
now because we know that they’ll be way

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:10.060
cheaper in 5 or 10 years, and that’s with
inflation.

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:14.529
If there was no inflation, it would just be
even more beneficial to wait it out.

00:09:14.529 --> 00:09:18.389
And as more people put of such purchases,
their savings will go up which according to

00:09:18.389 --> 00:09:21.199
the paradox of thrift is detrimental to economic
growth.

00:09:21.199 --> 00:09:26.269
So, one of the key reasons the Fed supports
inflation is to maximize GDP.

00:09:26.269 --> 00:09:28.660
Another key reason is to decrease the debt
load.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:33.360
It’s no secret that the federal government
has a boat load of debt, and inflation significantly

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:34.680
helps with handling this debt.

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:38.390
Here’s the thing, over time, debt payments
remain constant.

00:09:38.390 --> 00:09:42.899
However, with inflation, it becomes easier
and easier to earn money which in turn means

00:09:42.899 --> 00:09:45.540
that it becomes easier and easier to make
your monthly payment.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:50.639
Now of course, the government is still taking
on way too much debt, but that’s a whole

00:09:50.639 --> 00:09:51.639
different story.

00:09:51.639 --> 00:09:55.000
By maintaining low but persistent inflation,
they’re able to ease the burden of debt

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:58.630
payments not just for themselves but for everyone
in the US who’s in debt.

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:01.889
If you’re not willing to play the game how
they want it though by investing and taking

00:10:01.889 --> 00:10:05.060
on good debt, inflation is just constantly
screwing you over.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:11.290
THE FED EXPLAINED:
At the end of the day, money printing and

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:14.470
inflation are not that great for the average
person.

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:18.620
It makes it way harder to carry on daily life
and nearly impossible to achieve things like

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:19.770
home ownership.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:23.810
But, despite this, the fed wont stop printing
money anytime soon.

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:28.899
Sure, they might pull back for a little until
inflation cools down, but sooner or later,

00:10:28.899 --> 00:10:31.589
they’ll be printing money once again because
it protects national interests.

00:10:31.589 --> 00:10:37.251
It boosts GDP, makes the debt burden easier
to handle, and it allows the government to

00:10:37.251 --> 00:10:39.529
make up for their massive deficit.

00:10:39.529 --> 00:10:44.180
As an everyday person, we can complain about
this all we want, or we can play the game

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:45.180
how they want it.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:48.660
We can invest in stocks, real estate, leverage
low interest rates, and protect ourselves

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:50.970
as much as possible from inflation.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:54.639
Or we can do nothing and remain a part of
the middle class that’s constantly getting

00:10:54.639 --> 00:10:55.899
poorer and poorer.

00:10:55.899 --> 00:11:00.000
To me, the choice is clear, but that’s just
what I think.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.839
Would you guys prefer 0 inflation?

00:11:01.839 --> 00:11:03.120
Comment that down below.

